<h1>spiral storage</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="dynamicHashing.html"><em>dynamic hashing</em></a> table that grows a few slots at a time.  It uses a <a href="hash.html"><em>hash function</em></a>, h, with a <a href="range.html"><em>range</em></a> of [0,1).  For a key, k, an intermediate value, x=&lceil; S-h(k)&rceil; +h(k), is computed to find the final slot, &lfloor; d<sup>x</sup>&rfloor;, where d&gt;1 is called the growth factor.  To increase the number of slots, increase S to S' and rehash any keys from  &lfloor; d<sup>S</sup>&rfloor; to &lfloor; d<sup>S'</sup>&rfloor;-1.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="dynamicHashing.html"><em>dynamic hashing</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="linearHashing.html"><em>linear hashing</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Choosing d=2 and S=log<sub>2</sub>N, the number of slots, every expansion retires one slot and creates two new slots.  Since slots in use go from &lfloor; d<sup>S</sup>&rfloor; to  &lfloor; d<sup>S+1</sup>&rfloor;-1, they are usually remapped to physical storage.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>G. N. N. Martin</strong>, <em>Spiral storage: Incrementally augmentable hash addressed storage</em>, Theory of Computation Rep. 27, Univ. of Warwick, England, 1979. </p> <p> <strong>Per-&Aring;ke Larson</strong>, <em>Dynamic Hash Tables</em>, CACM 31(4):446-457, April 1988.
</p>

